Title: The Last Rehearsal
Credit: Written by
Author: Cesare Demo
Draft date: 2026-09-10

INT. COMMUNITY THEATER - STAGE - NIGHT

Bare bulbs on a work light. A half-built set: one flat, painted as a kitchen wall, leans against a ladder. MAYA (28), house manager and reluctant director, stands center stage holding a clipboard she is not reading.

Folding chairs face her, empty except for one.

DEREK (50s), the theater's aging technical director, sits with his arms crossed.

MAYA
(to no one)
Okay. So that's everyone.

DEREK
That's everyone.

MAYA
Three people signed up for the lead.

DEREK
Two backed out.

MAYA
(flat)
Two backed out.

She looks at the empty chairs, then at the painted kitchen wall.

MAYA (CONT'D)
We open in nine days, Derek.

DEREK
I know when we open.

MAYA
We have a set that's one wall, a script nobody's off-book on, and a cast of --

She stops. Counts on her fingers.

MAYA (CONT'D)
-- four, if I count myself, and I am not supposed to be in this show.

Derek unfolds his arms. Stands, slowly, joints audibly protesting.

DEREK
You want my honest opinion?

MAYA
Not really.

DEREK
(ignoring her)
Cancel it. Refund the six tickets we've sold. Nobody remembers a play that didn't happen.

Maya turns the clipboard over in her hands. On the back, taped, is a faded photo: an older woman in stage makeup, mid-bow, grinning at an unseen audience.

MAYA
(quiet)
My mother remembers every play that didn't happen. She used to list them. Like scars.

Derek softens, just slightly.

DEREK
Maya --

MAYA
(cutting him off, brighter)
We're not canceling. We're doing it with four people and one wall.

She sets the clipboard down on a folding chair.

MAYA (CONT'D)
Get the light on the wall. I want to see what we're working with.

Derek hesitates, then crosses to the light board. A beat. The work light dims, and a single warm spot rises on the kitchen wall -- suddenly it looks like a real room, half-finished but alive.

Maya watches the light change. Something in her posture shifts.

MAYA (CONT'D)
(to herself)
Okay. Okay, that's something.

INT. COMMUNITY THEATER - BACKSTAGE - CONTINUOUS

A cramped hallway of costume racks and old set pieces. JULES (19), a nervous first-year stage manager, hovers by a call sheet taped to the wall, phone in hand.

JULES
(into phone, whispering)
No, I know it's a mess, I'm just saying maybe don't tell your mom I said that --

Maya appears in the doorway. Jules jumps, nearly drops the phone.

JULES (CONT'D)
(to phone)
I have to go.

She hangs up fast.

JULES (CONT'D)
Sorry. That was -- nobody. Production stuff.

MAYA
(gently)
Jules. It's fine.

Beat.

MAYA (CONT'D)
Was that your mom?

JULES
(caught)
...She wanted to know if the show was still happening.

MAYA
What did you tell her?

JULES
I said I didn't know yet.

Maya considers this. Looks back toward the stage, where the warm light still glows on the half-built wall.

MAYA
Tell her it is.

Jules blinks.

JULES
Is it?

MAYA
(small, certain smile)
Ask me again in nine days.
